<section xml:id="section-activity-clo19">
  <title>Activity for CLO-19</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-clo19-review">
    <title>Conceptual Review</title>
    <p>
      You can test your learning from the video (and your own
      background knowledge) with the following review questions.
      Conceptual questions on quizes may be taken from these review
      questions. 
    </p>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is an initial value problem?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          An initial value problem is a differential equation along
          with an initial condition. Solving a differential equation
          will produce a function, but there will always be an unknown
          constant. A differential equation shows how a function
          grows, but where the growth starts. Therefore, an additional
          piece of information is required: an initial condition. This
          initial condition is used to solve for the unknown constant
          in the function. The result, then, is a unique solution to
          the differential equation determined by the initial
          condition. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is a separable equation?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          A separable equation is a differential equation where the
          left side is an isolated derivative and the right side is a
          product of two expressions. One of the expressions only
          involves the dependent variable and the other only involves
          the independent variable. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why is the substitution rule required so solve separable
          equations?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          After the equation has been separated, I integrate both side
          in the independent variable. However, the left side of the
          equation is an expression in the dependent variable. I want
          to change the variable, in order to use the dependent
          variable as the variable of integration. The substitution
          rules allows me to do this, changing the variable of
          integration. The result is just two single-variable
          integrals with no mixing of the variables. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why does percentage growth result in an exponential
          function?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Percentage growth is a differential equation. That
          differential equation is separable and can be solved. The
          solution to the differential equation is an exponential
          function. Therefore, percentage growth results in an
          exponential growth function.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-activity-separable-equations">
    <title>Calculation Questions: Separable Equations</title>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve this initial value problem.
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = x^2 y^2 
              \amp \amp y(0) = 1
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          I use the form for separable equations, with <m>f(x) =
          x^2</m> and <m>g(y) = y^2</m>.
          <md>
            \int \frac{1}{y^2} dy = \int x^2 dx 
          </md>
          Then I integrate both sides, add the constant of integration
          on the right.
          <md>
            \frac{-1}{y} = \frac{x^3}{3} + c= \frac{x^3+3c}{3} 
          </md>
          Then I solve for <m>y</m>.
          <md>
            y =  \frac{-3}{x^3+3c} 
          </md>
          Now I put the initial condition values in for <m>x</m> and
          <m>y</m>, to try to solve for the constant of integration.
          <md>
            1 = \frac{-3}{x + 3c} \implies c = -1 
          </md>
          Finally, having solve for the constant of integration, I
          replace <m>c</m> with the value solved for to get the final,
          unique solution.
          <md>
            y = \frac{-3}{x^3 -3} = \frac{3}{3-x^3} 
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve this initial value problem.
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = e^x e^{-y} 
              \amp \amp y(0) = 1 
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          I use the form for separable equations, with <m>f(x) =
          e^x</m> and <m>g(y) = e^{-y}</m>.
          <md>
            \int e^y dy = \int e^x dx 
          </md>
          Then I integrate both sides, add the constant of integration
          on the right.
          <md>
            e^y = e^x + c 
          </md>
          Then I solve for <m>y</m>.
          <md>
            y = \ln (e^x + c) 
          </md>
          Now I put the initial condition values in for <m>x</m> and
          <m>y</m>, to try to solve for the constant of integration.
          <md>
            1 = \ln (e^0 + c) = \ln (1+c) \implies c = e-1 
          </md>
          Finally, having solve for the constant of integration, I
          replace <m>c</m> with the value solved for to get the final,
          unique solution.
          <md>
            y = \ln (e^x + e - 1)
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve this initial value problem.
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = \frac{y}{x} 
              \amp \amp y(3) = 1
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          I use the form for separable equations, with <m>f(x) =
          \frac{1}{x}</m> and <m>g(y) = y</m>.
          <md>
            \int \frac{1}{y} dy = \int \frac{1}{x} dx 
          </md>
          Then I integrate both sides, add the constant of integration
          on the right.
          <md>
            \ln |y| = \ln |x| + c 
          </md>
          Then I solve for <m>y</m>.
          <md>
            y = e^{\ln x + c} = (x)(e^c) 
          </md>
          Now I put the initial condition values in for <m>x</m> and
          <m>y</m>, to try to solve for the constant of integration.
          <md>
            1 = 3(e^c) \implies (e^c) = \frac{1}{3} \implies c = \ln
            \frac{1}{3} = -\ln 3 
          </md>
          Finally, having solve for the constant of integration, I
          replace <m>c</m> with the value solved for to get the final,
          unique solution.
          <md>
            y = x e^{-\ln 3} = \frac{x}{e^{\ln 3}} = \frac{x}{3}
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve this initial value problem.
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = \sqrt{\frac{x}{y}} 
              \amp \amp y(0) = 0 
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The expression in the differential equation is not defined
          for this initial value. Therefore, this IVP cannot be
          solved. I could do the separable equation, but I won't be
          able to input the initial conditions.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve this initial value problem.
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = y^2 \sin x  
              \amp \amp y(\pi) = 1 
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          I use the form for separable equations, with <m>f(x) =
          \sin x^2</m> and <m>g(y) = y^2</m>.
          <md>
            \int \frac{1}{y^2} dy = \int \sin x dx 
          </md>
          Then I integrate both sides, add the constant of integration
          on the right.
          <md>
            \frac{-1}{y} = -\cos x + c 
          </md>
          Then I solve for <m>y</m>.
          <md>
            y = \frac{-1}{c - \cos x} = \frac{1}{\cos x - c} 
          </md>
          Now I put the initial condition values in for <m>x</m> and
          <m>y</m>, to try to solve for the constant of integration.
          <md>
            1 = \frac{1}{\cos \pi - c} = \frac{1}{-1 - c} \implies c= -2
          </md>
          Finally, having solve for the constant of integration, I
          replace <m>c</m> with the value solved for to get the final,
          unique solution.
          <md>
            y = \frac{1}{\cos x + 2}
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve this initial value problem.
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = \frac{(x-1)^2}{y} 
              \amp \amp y(2) = 1 
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          I use the form for separable equations, with <m>f(x) =
          (x-1)2</m> and <m>g(y) = \frac{1}{y}</m>.
          <md>
            \int y dy = \int (x-1)^2 dx 
          </md>
          Then I integrate both sides, add the constant of integration
          on the right.
          <md>
            \frac{y^2}{2} = \frac{(x-1)^3}{3} + c 
          </md>
          Then I solve for <m>y</m>.
          <md>
            \frac{y^2}{2} = \frac{(x-1)^3+3c}{3} \implies y =
            \sqrt{\frac{2(x-1)^3+6c}{3}} 
          </md>
          Now I put the initial condition values in for <m>x</m> and
          <m>y</m>, to try to solve for the constant of integration.
          <md>
            1 = \sqrt{\frac{2(2-1)^3+6c}{3}} \implies 3 = 2+6c \implies
            c = \frac{1}{6}
          </md>
          Finally, having solve for the constant of integration, I
          replace <m>c</m> with the value solved for to get the final,
          unique solution.
          <md>
            y = \sqrt{ \frac{2(x-1)^3+1}{3}}
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve this initial value problem.
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = x^3 y^4 
              \amp \amp y(1) = -3 
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          I use the form for separable equations, with <m>f(x) =
          x^3</m> and <m>g(y) = y^4</m>.
          <md>
            \int \frac{1}{y^4} dy = \int x^3 dx 
          </md>
          Then I integrate both sides, add the constant of integration
          on the right.
          <md>
            \frac{-1}{3y^3} = \frac{x^4}{4} + c = \frac{x^4 + 4c}{4}
          </md>
          Then I solve for <m>y</m>.
          <md>
            -3y^3 = \frac{4}{x^4 + 4c} \implies y^3 =
            \frac{-4}{3(x^4+4c)} \implies y = \sqrt[3]{\frac{-4}{3x^4 +
            12c}} 
          </md>
          Now I put the initial condition values in for <m>x</m> and
          <m>y</m>, to try to solve for the constant of integration.
          <md>
            -3 = \sqrt[3]{\frac{-4}{3(1)^4+ 12c}} \implies -27 =
            \frac{-4}{3+12c} 
          </md>
          <md>
            \frac{1}{27} = \frac{3+12c}{4} \implies 3+12c =
            \frac{4}{27} \implies 12c = \frac{-77}{27} \implies c =
            \frac{-77}{324} = 
          </md>
          Finally, having solve for the constant of integration, I
          replace <m>c</m> with the value solved for to get the final,
          unique solution.
          <md>
            y = \sqrt[3]{\frac{-4}{3x^4 + 12\frac{-77}{324}}} =
            \sqrt[3]{\frac{-4}{3x^2 - \frac{77}{27}}} = 
            \sqrt{3}{\frac{-108}{81x^2 - 77}} = 
            \sqrt{3}{\frac{108}{77 - 81x^2}} 
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve this initial value problem.
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = y^2 e^x 
              \amp \amp y(\ln 3) = 2 
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          I use the form for separable equations, with <m>f(x) =
          e^x</m> and <m>g(y) = y^2</m>.
          <md>
            \int \frac{1}{y^2} dy = \int e^x dx 
          </md>
          Then I integrate both sides, add the constant of integration
          on the right.
          <md>
            \frac{-1}{y} = e^x + c 
          </md>
          Then I solve for <m>y</m>.
          <md>
            y = \frac{-1}{e^x + c} 
          </md>
          Now I put the initial condition values in for <m>x</m> and
          <m>y</m>, to try to solve for the constant of integration.
          <md>
            2 = \frac{-1}{3+c} \implies c= \frac{-7}{2} 
          </md>
          Finally, having solve for the constant of integration, I
          replace <m>c</m> with the value solved for to get the final,
          unique solution.
          <md>
            y = \frac{-1}{e^x - \frac{7}{2}} = \frac{2}{7 - 2e^x} 
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
</section>
